<!-- ============================================
     SECTION 1: GERMANY & USSR
     ============================================ -->
<section class="section sec-1" id="s1">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="section-num">PART 01 / 05</div>
      <h2>Germany <span class="hi">Reunites</span><br>and the Cold War <span class="hi-pink">Ends</span></h2>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="drop">The early 1990s began with a map changing shape. For almost half a century after World War II, Germany had been split into two countries: <strong>West Germany</strong>, allied with the United States and Western Europe, and <strong>East Germany</strong>, controlled by a communist government backed by the Soviet Union. Berlin itself had been divided, and the <button class="term" data-key="berlin_wall">Berlin Wall</button> stood like a concrete scar across the city.</p>

      <p>When the wall fell in November 1989, it did not automatically create one Germany again. But it made <button class="term" data-key="reunification">reunification</button> possible. On October 3, 1990, East Germany formally joined the Federal Republic of Germany, and Germany became one country again. That date is now celebrated as the <strong>Day of German Unity</strong>.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Germans celebrating reunification at the Brandenburg Gate">
      <figcaption>// Berliners celebrate at the Brandenburg Gate, 1990</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Then came an even larger collapse. The <button class="term" data-key="soviet_union">Soviet Union</button> had been one of the two superpowers of the <button class="term" data-key="cold_war">Cold War</button>, a communist state made up of multiple republics and ruled from Moscow. But by 1991, it was breaking apart. Economic problems, nationalist movements, political reforms, and a failed hard-line coup weakened the central government.</p>

      <p>The U.S. State Department notes that the failed August 1991 coup against Soviet leader <button class="term" data-key="gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</button> sealed the fate of the Soviet Union by weakening him and elevating <button class="term" data-key="yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</button> and democratic forces in Russia.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="img-pair reveal">
      <figure>
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The Soviet flag is lowered and replaced by the Russian flag">
        <figcaption>// The hammer and sickle comes down for the last time</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A fallen Soviet hammer and sickle monument on a Moscow street">
        <figcaption>// A symbol on the ground, Moscow, early 1990s</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>By December 1991, the Soviet Union was gone. Its republics became independent countries, including <strong>Russia, Ukraine, Belarus, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Georgia, Armenia, Kazakhstan,</strong> and others. The Cold War did not end with one final battle. It ended more strangely: with flags lowered, governments dissolved, borders redrawn, and people waking up in countries that had new names.</p>

      <p>For American students, the key point is this: the United States entered the 1990s as the world's strongest remaining <button class="term" data-key="superpower">superpower</button>. But that did not mean history had ended. It meant the old villain in the old story had vanished, and nobody was fully sure what the next story would be.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reflect reveal">
      <h3>What Just Changed?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A country (East Germany) joined another country (West Germany) without a war. How does that change what borders mean?</li>
        <li>The Soviet Union ended without a final military defeat. What does that tell you about how big systems can fall apart?</li>
        <li>If the United States was now the only superpower left, what new responsibilities or risks might that create?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="retain reveal">
      <h3>Lock It In</h3>
      <p><strong>November 1989:</strong> Berlin Wall falls. <strong>October 3, 1990:</strong> Germany reunifies. <strong>December 1991:</strong> Soviet Union dissolves.</p>
      <div class="keywords">
        <span class="kw">REUNIFICATION</span>
        <span class="kw">COLD WAR ENDS</span>
        <span class="kw">SUPERPOWER</span>
        <span class="kw">GORBACHEV</span>
        <span class="kw">YELTSIN</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================
     SECTION 2: WORLD WIDE WEB
     ============================================ -->
<section class="section sec-2" id="s2">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="section-num">PART 02 / 05</div>
      <h2>The <span class="hi-cyan">World Wide Web</span><br>and the First Digital <span class="hi">Doorway</span></h2>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="drop">While the political map changed, another map was being drawn. Not on paper, but through wires, servers, screens, and code. The <button class="term" data-key="www">World Wide Web</button> was invented by British scientist <button class="term" data-key="berners_lee">Tim Berners-Lee</button> at <button class="term" data-key="cern">CERN</button> in 1989. CERN explains that the Web was first designed so scientists at universities and research institutes could share information quickly. In 1991, Berners-Lee released the World Wide Web software, opening the door for broader use.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Tim Berners-Lee at his workstation at CERN with the early World Wide Web on screen">
      <figcaption>// Tim Berners-Lee, the man who invented the Web</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>It is important to define this carefully. The <button class="term" data-key="internet">internet</button> and the World Wide Web are not exactly the same thing. The internet is the larger network of connected computers. The Web is a system that uses websites, links, web pages, browsers, and addresses to help people move through information. Think of the internet as the roads and wires. Think of the Web as the signs, buildings, doors, and maps that let ordinary people travel through it.</p>

      <p>At first, the Web did not look like the colorful internet most people know today. Early web pages were plain, text-heavy, and used mainly by researchers and technical users. But the idea was enormous: information could be linked. A person could click from one page to another through a <button class="term" data-key="hyperlink">hyperlink</button>. Knowledge could become a web instead of a stack.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-right">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A visualization of the early World Wide Web showing nodes connecting major early sites">
      <figcaption>// A map of the early Web. Every node a page, every line a link.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>This changed the meaning of distance. A bookstore, a university, a newspaper, a fan page, a government document, and a child's curiosity could eventually meet on the same glowing screen. In the early 1990s, most families did not yet live online. But the doorway had opened.</p>

      <p style="font-family: 'VT323', monospace; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--cyan); text-align: center; padding: 1.5rem 0;">&gt; The future was waiting behind a blue hyperlink.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reflect reveal" style="background: var(--lime); box-shadow: 10px 10px 0 var(--cyan);">
      <h3>Think It Through</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Why did the early Web look so plain, and what would it take for it to become interesting to non-scientists?</li>
        <li>The chapter calls the internet "the roads" and the Web "the signs and doors." What other everyday system follows that same pattern?</li>
        <li>What is one thing in your life right now that would be completely different if Tim Berners-Lee had never built the Web?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="retain reveal" style="background: var(--cyan); box-shadow: 10px 10px 0 var(--lime);">
      <h3>Lock It In</h3>
      <p><strong>1989:</strong> Web invented at CERN. <strong>1991:</strong> Web software released publicly. <strong>Internet ≠ Web.</strong> One is the network of computers, the other is how humans navigate it.</p>
      <div class="keywords">
        <span class="kw">HYPERLINK</span>
        <span class="kw">CERN</span>
        <span class="kw">BERNERS-LEE</span>
        <span class="kw">BROWSER</span>
        <span class="kw">DIGITAL DOORWAY</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

