<!-- ============================================
     SECTION 3: CLINTON & NAFTA
     ============================================ -->
<section class="section sec-3" id="s3">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="section-num">PART 03 / 05</div>
      <h2><span class="hi">Clinton</span>, <span class="hi-cyan">NAFTA</span>,<br>and the Politics of the<br>Post-Cold War World</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="drop">In 1992, <button class="term" data-key="clinton">Bill Clinton</button>, the Democratic governor of Arkansas, ran for president against incumbent Republican <strong>George H. W. Bush</strong> and independent candidate <button class="term" data-key="perot">Ross Perot</button>. Clinton argued that after the Cold War, the country needed to focus more on domestic problems such as <button class="term" data-key="recession">recession</button>, health care, jobs, education, and economic change. The White House Historical Association notes that Clinton won the three-way election with only 43 percent of the popular vote but a strong <button class="term" data-key="electoral_college">Electoral College</button> victory.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-left">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Bill Clinton speaking during the 1992 presidential campaign">
      <figcaption>// Governor Bill Clinton on the 1992 campaign trail</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Clinton represented a different kind of Democrat from earlier New Deal or Great Society liberals. He supported some government action but also accepted parts of the market-focused language of the Reagan era. He spoke about opportunity, responsibility, welfare reform, crime, education, and globalization. His politics reflected the 1990s mood: less Cold War ideology, more concern about trade, technology, middle-class life, and economic competition.</p>

      <p>One major example was <button class="term" data-key="nafta">NAFTA</button>, the North American Free Trade Agreement. NAFTA was signed in 1992, went into effect in 1994, and reduced many trade barriers among the United States, Canada, and Mexico. Britannica describes it as a controversial trade pact that gradually eliminated most <button class="term" data-key="tariffs">tariffs</button> and other trade barriers among the three countries.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="img-pair reveal">
      <figure>
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A graphic defining NAFTA showing arrows connecting the U.S., Canada, and Mexico">
        <figcaption>// NAFTA, defined</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Flags of the United States, Canada, and Mexico flying together">
        <figcaption>// Three flags, one agreement</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Supporters argued that NAFTA would increase trade, help businesses grow, lower prices, and connect North America economically. Critics argued it could move jobs out of the United States, weaken labor protections, pressure wages, and damage some local industries. Both sides were arguing about the same large force: <button class="term" data-key="globalization">globalization</button>, meaning the growing connection of economies, companies, workers, goods, and markets across national borders.</p>

      <p style="font-family: 'Bungee', sans-serif; font-size: 1.4rem; color: var(--purple-deep); text-align: center; padding: 2rem 0 1rem; line-height: 1.4;">The Cold War had asked, "Capitalism or communism?"<br>The 1990s asked a different question:<br><span style="color: var(--pink-hot);">"What kind of capitalism, and who benefits from it?"</span></p>
    </div>

    <div class="reflect reveal">
      <h3>Wrestle With It</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>If Clinton won with only 43% of the popular vote, what does that tell you about voters in 1992?</li>
        <li>Why might a trade agreement be popular with some Americans and unpopular with others, even at the same time?</li>
        <li>The chapter says the 1990s asked "What kind of capitalism, and who benefits from it?" How is that still being asked today?</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="retain reveal">
      <h3>Lock It In</h3>
      <p><strong>1992:</strong> Clinton wins, NAFTA signed. <strong>1994:</strong> NAFTA takes effect. The post-Cold War debate shifts from ideology to trade, jobs, and globalization.</p>
      <div class="keywords">
        <span class="kw">NAFTA</span>
        <span class="kw">GLOBALIZATION</span>
        <span class="kw">TARIFFS</span>
        <span class="kw">43 PERCENT</span>
        <span class="kw">ELECTORAL COLLEGE</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================
     SECTION 4: POP CULTURE
     ============================================ -->
<section class="section sec-4" id="s4">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="section-num">PART 04 / 05</div>
      <h2>Cartoon Network, <span class="hi-pink">Beanie Babies</span>,<br>Amazon, and the New<br><span class="hi">Consumer Spell</span></h2>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="drop">The early 1990s had a strange cultural texture. It was half old television world, half new internet world. Cable channels multiplied. Toys became collector objects. Sitcoms turned apartments into fantasy homes. Online commerce began as a small experiment and became something much larger.</p>

      <p>On October 1, 1992, <button class="term" data-key="cartoon_network">Cartoon Network</button> launched. It grew out of Turner Broadcasting's library of animation, including classic cartoons and Hanna-Barbera material. Early Cartoon Network helped make animation feel like its own full-time television universe rather than just Saturday morning programming.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap reveal">
      <div class="video-frame">
        <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/Q-HTHfiGOvc" title="The launch of Cartoon Network, 1992" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>In 1993, <button class="term" data-key="beanie_babies">Beanie Babies</button> appeared. The original nine included <strong>Legs the Frog, Squealer the Pig, Spot the Dog, Flash the Orca, Splash the Whale, Chocolate the Moose, Patti the Platypus, Brownie/Cubbie the Bear,</strong> and <strong>Pinchers the Lobster</strong>. At first, they were small stuffed animals with beans inside. But their limited releases, retirements, and tag variations later helped create a collector craze. A toy became a tiny market. People did not only hug them. They speculated on them.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-right">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Shoppers crowd a store counter trying to buy Beanie Babies during the collector craze">
      <figcaption>// The Beanie Baby line: half toy store, half stock market</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Then, on July 5, 1994, <button class="term" data-key="bezos">Jeff Bezos</button> founded the company that became <button class="term" data-key="amazon">Amazon</button>. It began as an online bookstore, not yet the giant "everything store" it later became. In the 1990s, the idea of buying books through a computer still felt unusual to many people. But Amazon showed how the Web could become a marketplace, not just a library.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-left">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="An early photograph of Amazon's small original office with the logo on the wall">
      <figcaption>// Amazon, when it still fit in one small room</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>That same year, <strong>Friends</strong> premiered on NBC on September 22, 1994. The show followed six young adults in New York City and became one of the defining sitcoms of the decade. Its world was cozy, witty, urban, and strangely affordable-looking. Like many cultural products of the 1990s, it turned uncertainty into comfort. The characters were not fighting the Cold War or waiting in gas lines. They were drinking coffee, dating badly, working awkward jobs, and trying to become adults together.</p>

      <p style="font-family: 'Bungee', sans-serif; font-size: 1.6rem; color: var(--pink-hot); text-align: center; padding: 1.5rem 0;">The 1990s were learning how to sell belonging.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal tilt-right" style="max-width: 480px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="The 1990s New York City skyline with the Twin Towers and the Brooklyn Bridge">
