<!-- ============================================
     SECTION 5: TOY STORY & RE-ELECT
     ============================================ -->
<section class="section sec-5" id="s5">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="section-num" style="background: var(--ink); color: var(--lime); box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--purple);">PART 05 / 05</div>
      <h2><span class="hi-pink">Toy Story</span>, Clinton Re-Elected,<br>and the New <span class="hi-cyan">Shape</span> of the Future</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p class="drop">In 1995, another door opened. This one inside a child's bedroom. <button class="term" data-key="toy_story">Toy Story</button>, released in theaters on November 22, 1995, was the world's first fully computer-generated animated feature film. <button class="term" data-key="pixar">Pixar</button> describes it as a release that permanently changed the animation industry and the future of the studio.</p>

      <p>The story was simple enough for children: toys come alive when humans are not looking. But the technology behind it was revolutionary. Animation no longer had to be drawn frame by frame in the traditional way. A whole film could be built with digital models, lighting, movement, and rendering.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-right">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="A still from Toy Story showing Woody in Andy's bedroom">
      <figcaption>// Woody, in a room rendered entirely by computer</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>This matters because Toy Story belongs to the same larger story as the Web, Amazon, and personal computing. The early 1990s were not only creating new gadgets. They were changing the way stories, shopping, work, communication, and imagination could be made.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal" style="max-width: 460px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Steve Jobs in the mid-1990s, around the era of Pixar and his return to Apple">
      <figcaption>// Steve Jobs, who backed Pixar before the world believed in it</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Then, in 1996, Bill Clinton was re-elected president. Strictly speaking, that belongs to the middle of the decade more than the early 1990s, but it completes this chapter. Clinton defeated Republican <button class="term" data-key="dole">Bob Dole</button> and Reform Party candidate Ross Perot, winning 379 electoral votes to Dole's 159. His reelection showed that many voters accepted his mix of moderate Democratic politics, economic growth, globalization, and post-Cold War optimism.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="reveal full tilt-left">
      <img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="President Bill Clinton speaking at a podium with the slogan 'Building America's Bridge to the 21st Century' behind him">
      <figcaption>// "Building America's Bridge to the 21st Century"</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>But the early 1990s were not simply cheerful. The collapse of the Soviet Union created instability as well as freedom. Globalization created winners and losers. The Web promised openness but would later bring misinformation, surveillance, and massive corporate power. NAFTA expanded trade but intensified arguments over labor and industry. Pop culture felt bright, but beneath it were economic anxieties, racial tensions, crime debates, and political polarization still taking shape.</p>

      <p>So the early 1990s stand like a bridge after a storm. Behind them: the Berlin Wall, the Soviet flag, Cold War fear. Ahead: the internet age, digital animation, online shopping, global trade, cable worlds, sitcom comfort, and a new kind of American confidence.</p>

      <p style="font-family: 'Monoton', cursive; font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); text-align: center; padding: 2rem 0 1rem; line-height: 1.05; color: var(--ink); text-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--paper);">The old map had burned.<br>The new one was loading.</p>
    </div>

